(822673938f8ef1b79f8bda3b852adef0fe2c6055src/SpaceEngineersVR/2cheezecake-dev/CheezecakeSEVR:z
openxr_loaderlib/openxr/openxr_loader.dll"@66946ea6e9cc2649e299ec412521c5eafe56c635d05055097fe4db81e11932df(JWindows
$600D4D7F-D4FE-41F4-8A35-CB848B8B3E3FCheezecake's SE VR*TEarly access: play Space Engineers in a VR headset, with your hands, through OpenXR.2
Cheezecake:›
EARLY ACCESS. Play Space Engineers 1 in a PC VR headset. The game's own renderer draws each eye, through OpenXR: no VorpX, ReShade or other injector.

- Tools sit in your hands and aim where you point; hold rifles and tools with both hands
- Hold the stick and throttle to fly; press buttons with a fingertip; grab seats, doors and terminals to use them
- Climb along hulls and ladders by hand; open your helmet by gripping it
- Tools wheel, actions wheel, a wrist panel, and your suit status on your wrist
- Standing or seated play, with height calibration; dynamic resolution

To start: connect the headset first, start the game with Pulsar's Legacy launcher, and hold the left Menu button to recentre. Settings are in Options > VR.

Linux (native, no Proton): start the game with Pulsar's Interim launcher. It needs the distribution's OpenXR loader, WiVRn and, for now, a patched DXVK Native; the README has the steps.

Early access: tested only on a Quest 3 over Meta Link (cable) with an RTX 3060. Other OpenXR headsets and runtimes should work but are untested; Vive wands have their own layout, not yet tried on real wands. Meta advises that NVIDIA drivers newer than 591.86 can make Link freeze.

Controls, known issues and bug reports: https://github.com/cheezecake-dev/CheezecakeSEVR

A separate project, not related to Math0424's earlier SpaceEngineersVR plugin.BCLR,CoreCLRRWindows,Linux
